{
  "fixture": "duplicate-vouchers",
  "description": "F-CORE-044: the same document entered twice is the one bookkeeping defect summae could not see at all. `voucherNumber` is a free string with no uniqueness of any kind, so an incoming invoice booked a second time produces a second voucher, a second balanced entry and a SECOND INPUT-TAX DEDUCTION — and every invariant holds: the entries balance, both carry a voucher, both sit in an open period, the trial balance adds up. Nothing looked wrong anywhere; the money was simply claimed twice. WHY A PROJECTION AND NOT A REFUSAL, because that is the decision this fixture pins as much as the numbers: duplicate voucher numbers are legitimate across sources — two suppliers may both send their invoice number 1, and step 5 books exactly that — so the grouping is by DOCUMENT IDENTITY (issuer plus number) and even then the answer is a report. A uniqueness rule would be wrong in a way nobody could work around, and the only thing worse than a missing check is one that blocks correct bookkeeping; `vatReturn.gapWarnings` draws the same line. Three things are proven not to be findings: a different supplier with the same number (one voucher under its own identity, so no group), a `recurring` voucher repeating its number (a Dauerbeleg is what the flag means), and a duplicate that was already CORRECTED — its entry is reversed, so `postedTotal` reads 0.00 and `stillPosted` says one of the two still moves the books. The corrected one stays visible with its history rather than dropping out, because a list somebody reads to decide whether it was corrected must show that it was. The third group has no partner at all and is held together by `issuer`: the master record is the identity where there is one, the typed string where there is not.",
